lore-object-dispatch/migrations/*.sql text eol=lf

# A migration's bytes reach PostgreSQL verbatim, and PL/pgSQL function bodies
# are stored as text. On a CRLF checkout every trigger body installed from a
# migration file carries carriage returns that the same DDL declared as a Rust
# string literal does not, so `pg_get_functiondef` reports two schemas as
# different when only their line endings differ — and the difference is
# invisible in the failure output. That is what broke
# `domain_migration_parity`'s catalog comparison on Windows.
#
# Table, column, constraint, and index definitions are parsed and normalised by
# the server, so only function and trigger bodies expose this. A migration file
# that declares none can be CRLF for years and look fine.
lore-postgres/migrations/*.sql text eol=lf

# Rust sources are LF in history. Without this rule a Windows checkout can hold
# CRLF copies that `git status` still reports clean, and the next edit to such a
# file commits the whole file as CRLF (rustfmt then reformats every line).
*.rs text eol=lf

# Scoped to the fork-private object-dispatch protos, NOT to `*.proto`. Most of
# the workspace's proto blobs are CRLF in history because they are upstream
# Epic's, so a blanket `*.proto text eol=lf` would renormalise them on next
# touch and manufacture divergence against upstream. These files are ours, are
# LF in history, and a one-line edit to one of them already came back as a
# whole-file CRLF rewrite once.
lore-proto/proto/lore/object_dispatch/v1/*.proto text eol=lf
